1
00:00:16,588 --> 00:00:31,119
[SPEAKER_02]: So, this is a sutta on an astounding woman, an astounding mother, Nandamata, and she was apparently an anāgāmī.

2
00:00:31,519 --> 00:00:37,744
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the first five lower fetters have been completely destroyed.

3
00:00:42,467 --> 00:00:44,608
[SPEAKER_02]: So, these are about her astounding qualities.

4
00:00:46,129 --> 00:00:52,133
[SPEAKER_02]: And she's actually telling the great Venerable Sariputta about these qualities.

5
00:00:52,173 --> 00:00:53,674
[SPEAKER_02]: And he's saying, wow, that's amazing.

6
00:00:54,755 --> 00:00:56,095
[SPEAKER_02]: Wow, that's amazing, that's amazing.

7
00:00:57,957 --> 00:01:01,379
[SPEAKER_02]: And there's a few that stand out, really.

8
00:01:05,663 --> 00:01:19,995
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, the first one is that as she's doing some recitation or reciting some suttas, apparently, there is a heavenly being or a god that comes to speak to her and praise her for that.

9
00:01:21,696 --> 00:01:30,483
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the fact is that she... that gods, according to this, are coming to speak to her.

10
00:01:31,424 --> 00:01:33,285
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, she's not sort of going to them.

11
00:01:35,289 --> 00:01:47,857
[SPEAKER_02]: As a human being, so, which kind of shows the loftiness of her mind, I would say, you know, it's so beyond this world, where even she's conversing with gods.

12
00:01:54,021 --> 00:01:58,644
[SPEAKER_02]: And so Venerable Sariputta says, that's amazing, that's wonderful.

13
00:01:59,525 --> 00:02:04,048
[SPEAKER_02]: And she says, so this is not my only incredible and amazing quality, there is another.

14
00:02:06,106 --> 00:02:10,348
[SPEAKER_02]: I had an only son, called Nanda, who was dear and pleasing to me.

15
00:02:10,368 --> 00:02:17,131
[SPEAKER_02]: The rulers, the great authorities, forcibly abducted him on some pretext and had him executed.

16
00:02:18,191 --> 00:02:26,755
[SPEAKER_02]: But I can't recall getting upset when he was under arrest, being arrested, imprisoned or being put in prison, killed or being killed.

17
00:02:30,277 --> 00:02:32,798
[SPEAKER_02]: And Venerable Sariputta says that it's incredible.

18
00:02:34,089 --> 00:02:37,672
[SPEAKER_02]: It's amazing that you purify an arisen thought.

19
00:02:38,433 --> 00:02:41,596
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't quite understand that, but... Purify thought in arising.

20
00:02:41,696 --> 00:02:50,383
[SPEAKER_05]: As soon as the thought manifested, there's no time for it to accrue the unwholesomeness, the views, the leanings, the cravings.

21
00:02:51,224 --> 00:02:54,627
[SPEAKER_05]: It's just seen for what it is, as it is.

22
00:02:54,887 --> 00:02:55,508
[SPEAKER_05]: Most people don't.

23
00:02:55,528 --> 00:03:01,013
[SPEAKER_05]: Most people catch the thought down the stream, when it's already polluted with this or that, and then they have to undo it back.

24
00:03:02,546 --> 00:03:06,968
[SPEAKER_05]: But if the mind is developed, as soon as the thought arises, it's seen correctly, with right wisdom.

25
00:03:07,408 --> 00:03:08,469
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, this is unwholesome.

26
00:03:09,229 --> 00:03:11,010
[SPEAKER_02]: This should not be delighted in or pursued.

27
00:03:11,030 --> 00:03:13,511
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, I think that... even that's gone now.

28
00:03:13,731 --> 00:03:15,272
[SPEAKER_05]: She doesn't even need to say that anymore.

29
00:03:15,312 --> 00:03:17,733
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it's so amazing.

30
00:03:17,953 --> 00:03:24,336
[SPEAKER_05]: As it arises, it's being purified, so there is no... oh, and I shouldn't think these unwholesome thoughts.

31
00:03:24,636 --> 00:03:24,856
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

32
00:03:26,957 --> 00:03:29,518
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, you know what... you know disgusting as disgusting.

33
00:03:29,538 --> 00:03:30,779
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't have to keep telling yourself.

34
00:03:31,888 --> 00:03:32,709
[SPEAKER_02]: Unwholesome as unwholesome.

35
00:03:32,729 --> 00:03:33,349
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, exactly.

36
00:03:33,369 --> 00:03:38,634
[SPEAKER_05]: So, there is no more lapsing of the context in regard to the immediate arising of thoughts.

37
00:03:39,995 --> 00:03:41,997
[SPEAKER_05]: That's where the context penetrated through.

38
00:03:42,117 --> 00:03:42,357
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

39
00:03:43,158 --> 00:03:43,759
[SPEAKER_04]: Thoughts of what?

40
00:03:44,099 --> 00:03:45,200
[SPEAKER_04]: My son getting imprisoned?

41
00:03:45,240 --> 00:03:46,101
[SPEAKER_04]: Anything, anything, anything.

42
00:03:46,961 --> 00:03:53,407
[SPEAKER_05]: Thought of this, thought of that, thoughts that would give rise to sensuality, or ill-will, or sadness, or grief, or hindrance.

43
00:03:53,427 --> 00:03:54,028
[SPEAKER_05]: Doesn't matter.

44
00:03:54,608 --> 00:03:58,532
[SPEAKER_05]: In its nature, it's already purified before it could even give rise to such things.

45
00:03:59,426 --> 00:04:02,348
[SPEAKER_01]: It's seen as... it's seen as a thought in its nature.

46
00:04:02,428 --> 00:04:03,689
[SPEAKER_05]: It's understood in its nature, yeah.

47
00:04:04,010 --> 00:04:06,792
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, so she's... she can see her mind.

48
00:04:06,992 --> 00:04:08,253
[SPEAKER_02]: She's seeing the signs of the mind.

49
00:04:08,533 --> 00:04:10,094
[SPEAKER_05]: Oh, she's way past that point.

50
00:04:10,114 --> 00:04:11,235
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah.

51
00:04:11,515 --> 00:04:16,539
[SPEAKER_05]: So purifying the arising... You see the signs of your mind and then you can start purifying the thoughts correctly.

52
00:04:17,400 --> 00:04:20,142
[SPEAKER_05]: But she's already purifying the arising of the thoughts.

53
00:04:20,736 --> 00:04:22,859
[SPEAKER_05]: That's already affected by her understanding.

54
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:24,742
[SPEAKER_05]: So, she doesn't even need to purify her thoughts.

55
00:04:25,524 --> 00:04:27,507
[SPEAKER_05]: She's cleaning things at the top of the spring.

56
00:04:27,727 --> 00:04:28,589
[SPEAKER_05]: She's keeping it clean.

57
00:04:28,909 --> 00:04:29,630
[SPEAKER_05]: She's not even cleaning it.

58
00:04:29,650 --> 00:04:30,492
[SPEAKER_05]: She's keeping it clean.

59
00:04:31,775 --> 00:04:37,498
[SPEAKER_02]: So, someone said, well, she... the son, her son, she obviously didn't care.

60
00:04:38,338 --> 00:04:46,602
[SPEAKER_05]: She was so, like, dispassionate towards... Well, yeah, you can be cynical about everything, but she said herself, the son she loved, the son she raised, it was very pleasing to her.

61
00:04:47,243 --> 00:04:55,007
[SPEAKER_05]: So, if you have a reason to think that she was lying, sure, but Sariputta didn't think that, so, yeah.

62
00:04:55,427 --> 00:04:57,508
[SPEAKER_05]: I mean, you can also think, oh, she sounds very conceited.

63
00:04:58,501 --> 00:05:01,903
[SPEAKER_05]: Oh, yes, Venerable Sariputta, that's not the only amazing quality that I have.

64
00:05:01,943 --> 00:05:03,544
[SPEAKER_05]: Let me tell you more about myself.

65
00:05:04,404 --> 00:05:08,186
[SPEAKER_05]: I mean, to an uninstructed worldly, that can sound as the art of conceit and arrogance.

66
00:05:09,226 --> 00:05:10,347
[SPEAKER_05]: But it's just factually true.

67
00:05:10,907 --> 00:05:14,369
[SPEAKER_05]: These are things factually amazing, because dhamma is amazing.

68
00:05:14,749 --> 00:05:15,870
[SPEAKER_05]: And that's exactly what she says.

69
00:05:16,850 --> 00:05:18,251
[SPEAKER_05]: She practiced it, she developed it.

70
00:05:18,271 --> 00:05:18,891
[SPEAKER_05]: It's like, oh, wow!

71
00:05:19,251 --> 00:05:22,273
[SPEAKER_05]: Even she's surprised how amazing these things are once you practice them.

72
00:05:26,115 --> 00:05:28,016
[SPEAKER_00]: I think we're a lot of, you know, initially

73
00:05:29,362 --> 00:05:32,924
[SPEAKER_00]: It just sounds like, oh, but that's impossible.

74
00:05:32,944 --> 00:05:42,068
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, the only way that somebody could not care or not be moved by such an event is if they just become like a kind of a robot or something.

75
00:05:42,088 --> 00:05:42,228
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

76
00:05:42,248 --> 00:05:52,112
[SPEAKER_00]: They just don't have any emotion or feeling or any... they don't see the significance of things anymore, which is kind of why you can't do that work.

77
00:05:52,132 --> 00:05:52,993
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, they dismiss it.

78
00:05:53,273 --> 00:05:55,154
[SPEAKER_05]: They dismiss the possibility because it's just...

79
00:05:56,194 --> 00:05:57,855
[SPEAKER_05]: Incomprehensible how that's possible.

80
00:05:57,895 --> 00:05:59,016
[SPEAKER_05]: Thus, it's not possible.

81
00:05:59,256 --> 00:06:03,878
[SPEAKER_05]: Because my own limited thinking is still my reference for what's right and wrong.

82
00:06:04,318 --> 00:06:05,699
[SPEAKER_05]: That's most people, basically.

83
00:06:06,880 --> 00:06:10,902
[SPEAKER_02]: But she was, obviously, she clearly knew the significance of things.

84
00:06:11,142 --> 00:06:13,563
[SPEAKER_00]: My dear and pleasing son.

85
00:06:13,963 --> 00:06:24,549
[SPEAKER_05]: And well, again, she would not have listed that as an amazing thing, that her mind didn't move amidst this quite a horrible experience of having your son falsely imprisoned and killed.

86
00:06:25,329 --> 00:06:41,758
[SPEAKER_05]: If she did not understand and kind of experience that significance, the full weight, the full extent of having your own son abducted, tortured, killed, all at a false pretext, and you couldn't do anything about it, and yet her mind didn't move, that's the amazing aspect.

87
00:06:42,318 --> 00:06:43,839
[SPEAKER_05]: It's like, oh, I didn't care about that son.

88
00:06:43,979 --> 00:06:44,639
[SPEAKER_05]: I never saw him.

89
00:06:45,020 --> 00:06:45,860
[SPEAKER_05]: He was a drunkard.

90
00:06:46,360 --> 00:06:46,781
[SPEAKER_05]: He was killed.

91
00:06:46,801 --> 00:06:47,341
[SPEAKER_05]: I didn't care.

92
00:06:47,381 --> 00:06:49,162
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, that's not much of an accomplishment, isn't it?

93
00:06:49,282 --> 00:06:50,803
[SPEAKER_05]: You wouldn't list that as a great thing.

94
00:06:51,583 --> 00:06:56,066
[SPEAKER_05]: means she actually felt the full extent of that experience, yet remained unmoved.

95
00:06:57,466 --> 00:06:59,468
[SPEAKER_05]: And the dhamma is wonderful because of that.

96
00:06:59,568 --> 00:07:13,015
[SPEAKER_05]: And same that other man, who also understood the peril of sensuality, the full degree of it, went back home, after being with the Buddha and said, I, you know, the sexual intercourse is unthinkable for me anymore.

97
00:07:13,135 --> 00:07:15,917
[SPEAKER_05]: So, told his wives he won't... he'd be celibate for the rest of his life.

98
00:07:15,957 --> 00:07:16,958
[SPEAKER_05]: Basically, it was an anāgāmī.

99
00:07:18,057 --> 00:07:20,018
[SPEAKER_05]: If you want to stay here, I'll support you.

100
00:07:20,878 --> 00:07:24,020
[SPEAKER_05]: If you want to go and remarry, I'll support you, I'll set you up.

101
00:07:24,800 --> 00:07:28,742
[SPEAKER_05]: And then, his first wife says, okay, take me to such and such man.

102
00:07:29,822 --> 00:07:42,088
[SPEAKER_05]: He says... Now, you could think, oh, he didn't feel anything, he was a robot, but then he would not have described the whole thing and the significance of it, because he said, no, he took his first, his dearest wife, the one that was longest with him,

103
00:07:43,940 --> 00:07:58,869
[SPEAKER_05]: By her hand, took him to that man, joined their hands, poured the water over in that ceremony of giving the blessing, and he was surprised that his own mind didn't move at any part of that process, and then explained, isn't this dhamma wonderful?

104
00:07:59,309 --> 00:08:04,933
[SPEAKER_05]: Once you practice it and understand the danger of sensuality, your mind doesn't move amidst things that...

105
00:08:05,553 --> 00:08:06,654
[SPEAKER_05]: I'm surprised I didn't move.

106
00:08:07,975 --> 00:08:13,158
[SPEAKER_05]: So, he felt the full weight of it, yet it wasn't enough to move the mind that freed himself from it.

107
00:08:15,433 --> 00:08:19,116
[SPEAKER_05]: And that's why I say, like, you find a way out of suffering amidst the suffering.

108
00:08:19,476 --> 00:08:21,438
[SPEAKER_05]: And there is no greater verification than that.

109
00:08:22,339 --> 00:08:25,682
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's the one, yeah, for a mother... For a mother's son, yeah.

110
00:08:26,062 --> 00:08:27,463
[SPEAKER_05]: In his case, he was that...

111
00:08:27,503 --> 00:08:28,704
[SPEAKER_05]: There's always going to be something.

112
00:08:29,465 --> 00:08:33,408
[SPEAKER_05]: So, the question is not about destroying the significance of things.

113
00:08:33,428 --> 00:08:34,048
[SPEAKER_02]: There is no son.

114
00:08:34,068 --> 00:08:38,512
[SPEAKER_05]: Telling you, exactly, there is no son, there is no mother, there is no wife, there is no dearness, there is no...

115
00:08:38,532 --> 00:08:38,953
[SPEAKER_05]: There is.

116
00:08:40,033 --> 00:08:42,816
[SPEAKER_05]: Yet, you don't move by it, because you train your mind in regard to that.

117
00:08:44,242 --> 00:08:46,984
[SPEAKER_05]: But for most people, the idea of training is the idea of denial.

118
00:08:47,944 --> 00:08:52,627
[SPEAKER_05]: Oh, there is no... there's nothing real, and kalāpas are constantly flicking and changing, and atoms are flying.

119
00:08:52,987 --> 00:08:53,548
[SPEAKER_05]: Nothing is real.

120
00:08:53,608 --> 00:08:54,648
[SPEAKER_05]: Nothing is real in reality.

121
00:08:54,668 --> 00:08:56,770
[SPEAKER_05]: It's just these empty designations, conventions.

122
00:08:57,650 --> 00:08:58,791
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, good luck with that then.

123
00:08:59,831 --> 00:09:09,077
[SPEAKER_05]: Because when the confinement does come, and those secondary management explanations don't apply anymore, you're left with what you started, suffering, and not knowing the escape from it.

124
00:09:10,688 --> 00:09:14,530
[SPEAKER_04]: And the difference then would be basically seeing it here, or like that, basically.

125
00:09:15,411 --> 00:09:20,494
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, the difference would be recognizing that it doesn't come from the significance, from the things, from the experience.

126
00:09:20,554 --> 00:09:22,295
[SPEAKER_05]: It comes from your own resistance towards it.

127
00:09:23,612 --> 00:09:32,023
[SPEAKER_00]: People would regard that as being a bad parent, even if you were actually, you know, making some efforts to... but not being emotionally involved.

128
00:09:32,103 --> 00:09:33,785
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah, of course, of course, yeah.

129
00:09:33,805 --> 00:09:34,906
[SPEAKER_00]: That would be considered a bad parent.

130
00:09:34,926 --> 00:09:41,154
[SPEAKER_05]: Oh, that's all good, but you need to, like, the only way for you to show your love is to cry and to be emotionally disturbed.

131
00:09:41,795 --> 00:09:48,916
[SPEAKER_05]: But if you stop and look at it closely, the only reason people give in to tears and emotional disturbance is because they don't want to bear it.

132
00:09:49,676 --> 00:09:50,377
[SPEAKER_05]: They can't bear it.

133
00:09:50,997 --> 00:09:53,137
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it's a magical release of the pressure.

134
00:09:53,257 --> 00:09:57,318
[SPEAKER_05]: Just break down, cry, and then you'll feel better after the physical fatigue sets in.

135
00:09:57,918 --> 00:10:00,458
[SPEAKER_05]: So, you are actually loving that person less.

136
00:10:00,838 --> 00:10:03,599
[SPEAKER_05]: Because you cannot endure the extent of their significance.

137
00:10:04,319 --> 00:10:06,319
[SPEAKER_05]: But you break down like a little child and cry.

138
00:10:07,340 --> 00:10:09,420
[SPEAKER_05]: As a defense mechanism, not as a great love.

139
00:10:25,795 --> 00:10:37,419
[SPEAKER_05]: So, to take in the full significance of... Yeah, because that's like... you cry, you don't want to face it, you act, you kick, you scream when your mind moves.

140
00:10:38,319 --> 00:10:41,200
[SPEAKER_05]: And your mind moves when the mind can't bear the significance of it.

141
00:10:43,021 --> 00:10:45,361
[SPEAKER_05]: The pressure, the weight, the love, whatever it is.

142
00:10:46,202 --> 00:10:50,023
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it's correct to say that the only person they can

143
00:10:50,383 --> 00:10:59,246
[SPEAKER_05]: To truly see the extent of loving your son would be at least an anāgāmī, needs to be at least an anāgāmī, or have at least the right view.

144
00:11:00,426 --> 00:11:10,350
[SPEAKER_05]: And they'll be able to fully take the weight of someone's significance, presence, love, death, without moving, without fearing it.

145
00:11:10,730 --> 00:11:13,291
[SPEAKER_05]: They can see how important that person was to them.

146
00:11:14,382 --> 00:11:15,343
[SPEAKER_04]: Otherwise, they're just selfish.

147
00:11:15,403 --> 00:11:16,164
[SPEAKER_04]: That's why they're crying.

148
00:11:18,706 --> 00:11:19,827
[SPEAKER_05]: Contrary to popular belief.

149
00:11:20,147 --> 00:11:22,909
[SPEAKER_05]: It's basically difficult to remain unmoved.

150
00:11:22,989 --> 00:11:31,796
[SPEAKER_05]: And then, on top of it, you now have the view that says, no, no, no, you should be moved on account of love or whatever emotion you have.

151
00:11:31,977 --> 00:11:36,660
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, but then you realize, well, the only reason I say I should be moved is because I can't help but move.

152
00:11:37,421 --> 00:11:38,142
[SPEAKER_05]: If I'm honest,

153
00:11:39,225 --> 00:11:43,047
[SPEAKER_05]: At least if I were to have an option to not move, okay, then I can maybe decide to move or not.

154
00:11:43,087 --> 00:11:43,688
[SPEAKER_05]: But I don't have...

155
00:11:43,708 --> 00:11:44,228
[SPEAKER_05]: I just move.

156
00:11:44,608 --> 00:11:45,689
[SPEAKER_05]: Because it's just crushing me.

157
00:11:45,729 --> 00:11:46,550
[SPEAKER_05]: I just can't bear it.

158
00:11:47,510 --> 00:11:48,611
[SPEAKER_05]: That's how much I love you.

159
00:11:48,831 --> 00:11:52,934
[SPEAKER_05]: I... that's how much I care about avoiding discomfort in my own mind.

160
00:11:53,654 --> 00:11:54,875
[SPEAKER_05]: And people do understand that.

161
00:11:54,975 --> 00:11:59,418
[SPEAKER_05]: Like when they say, you know, people are greatly in love, it's like... it's a very selfish thing.

162
00:12:00,078 --> 00:12:04,521
[SPEAKER_05]: Like you basically almost objectify this person that represents that love for you.

163
00:12:05,362 --> 00:12:07,083
[SPEAKER_05]: Because that's how self-centered you are.

164
00:12:07,721 --> 00:12:14,344
[SPEAKER_05]: And yeah, you'll be distraught, you won't eat for a month, you'll be crying, if things... but all because you're so selfish, because you can't bear it.

165
00:12:14,444 --> 00:12:19,086
[SPEAKER_05]: You can't bear it on the mental level, so you're doing all these magical acts and breakdowns to cope with it.

166
00:12:19,967 --> 00:12:23,629
[SPEAKER_05]: You just don't want to face pain, just don't want to face it.

167
00:12:25,390 --> 00:12:26,310
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's what it is.

168
00:12:27,130 --> 00:12:28,051
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not able to.

169
00:12:29,220 --> 00:12:30,681
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, allow this pain to endure.

170
00:12:30,761 --> 00:12:35,803
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to, like, yeah, we have Hollywood and all these, you know, glamorizing the acting out of it.

171
00:12:35,823 --> 00:12:46,828
[SPEAKER_01]: But actually, yeah, if you just are, you know, in a way, like, friendly with this pain, then, yeah, you're able to see that it's possible to not have this obsession, to try and escape this pain.

172
00:12:47,089 --> 00:12:47,289
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

173
00:12:47,789 --> 00:12:49,390
[SPEAKER_05]: And then, you could then clearly...

174
00:12:50,050 --> 00:13:13,197
[SPEAKER_05]: With an unpolluted kind of context, see the significance of that person, whether it was just a sensual infatuation, obsession, whether it was genuinely a close friend or something, doesn't matter, because you would not be magically sort of inventing things to cover up the weight, because the mind's not moved, so the mind can remain facing it, seeing it.

175
00:13:14,310 --> 00:13:18,233
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that doesn't stop you from, I don't know, looking after them or helping them.

176
00:13:18,253 --> 00:13:18,513
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

177
00:13:18,714 --> 00:13:19,114
[SPEAKER_02]: Not at all.

178
00:13:19,454 --> 00:13:19,975
[SPEAKER_02]: Not at all.

179
00:13:19,995 --> 00:13:21,155
[SPEAKER_05]: Quite the opposite.

180
00:13:21,376 --> 00:13:23,838
[SPEAKER_05]: You will do it even better if you're not acting selfishly.

181
00:13:24,198 --> 00:13:27,000
